{"module":"slavery-law","voice":"male","duration":190.5,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Slavery makes race law","start":0.0,"end":2.04,"words":[[0.04,0.81],[0.81,1.29],[1.29,1.67],[1.67,1.87]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Slavery in America was not a Southern side story.","start":2.84,"end":6.73,"words":[[2.87,3.54],[3.54,3.7],[3.7,4.29],[4.29,4.54],[4.54,4.79],[4.79,4.95],[4.95,5.37],[5.37,5.7],[5.7,6.2]]},{"el":"intro","text":"It was written into the Constitution, financed by Northern banks, and was the country's largest financial asset by 1860.","start":7.08,"end":15.92,"words":[[7.12,7.28],[7.28,7.5],[7.5,7.89],[7.89,8.19],[8.19,8.34],[8.34,9.34],[9.6,10.22],[10.22,10.44],[10.44,10.83],[10.83,11.29],[11.55,11.78],[11.78,12.01],[12.01,12.16],[12.16,12.7],[12.7,13.23],[13.23,13.92],[13.92,14.23],[14.23,14.45],[14.45,15.37]]},{"el":"h0","text":"The trade","start":16.27,"end":17.05,"words":[[16.32,16.5],[16.5,16.95]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 12.5 million Africans were forced onto slave ships between the 1500s and 1866, and about 10.7 million survived the Middle Passage (SlaveVoyages.org).","start":17.75,"end":31.09,"words":[[17.8,18.19],[18.19,19.29],[19.29,19.76],[19.76,20.39],[20.39,20.55],[20.55,20.86],[20.86,21.17],[21.17,21.57],[21.57,21.88],[21.88,22.35],[22.35,22.51],[22.51,23.61],[23.61,23.85],[23.85,25.1],[25.38,25.61],[25.61,26.01],[26.01,27.03],[27.03,27.5],[27.5,28.05],[28.05,28.21],[28.21,28.6],[28.6,29.07],[29.07,30.56]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 388,000 were brought directly to mainland North America.","start":31.44,"end":34.84,"words":[[31.48,31.69],[31.69,32.84],[32.84,32.93],[32.93,33.1],[33.1,33.48],[33.48,33.57],[33.57,33.91],[33.91,34.03],[34.03,34.38]]},{"el":"s0","text":"After Congress banned importing enslaved people in 1808, a domestic trade forcibly moved about a million people from the Upper South to the cotton fields of the Deep South, tearing families apart.","start":35.19,"end":48.98,"words":[[35.24,35.58],[35.58,36.17],[36.17,36.51],[36.51,37.1],[37.1,37.77],[37.77,38.2],[38.2,38.36],[38.36,39.29],[39.59,39.76],[39.76,40.44],[40.44,40.86],[40.86,41.45],[41.45,41.79],[41.79,42.21],[42.21,42.38],[42.38,42.89],[42.89,43.31],[43.31,43.65],[43.65,43.82],[43.82,44.07],[44.07,44.41],[44.41,44.58],[44.58,44.75],[44.75,45.17],[45.17,45.59],[45.59,45.76],[45.76,45.93],[45.93,46.18],[46.18,46.61],[46.9,47.41],[47.41,48.0],[48.0,48.42]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Built into the nation's economy","start":49.88,"end":52.2,"words":[[49.95,50.3],[50.3,50.64],[50.64,50.81],[50.81,51.42],[51.42,52.03]]},{"el":"s1","text":"By 1860, about 3.95 million people were enslaved in the U.S. Their market value as “property” was greater than that of all the country's railroads and factories combined.","start":52.9,"end":65.85,"words":[[52.95,53.19],[53.19,54.15],[54.43,54.83],[54.83,56.11],[56.11,56.59],[56.59,56.99],[56.99,57.15],[57.15,57.79],[57.79,57.95],[57.95,58.11],[58.11,58.51],[58.91,59.15],[59.15,59.55],[59.55,59.95],[59.95,60.11],[60.11,60.67],[60.67,60.91],[60.91,61.39],[61.39,61.63],[61.63,61.87],[61.87,62.03],[62.03,62.19],[62.19,62.35],[62.35,62.91],[62.91,63.63],[63.63,63.87],[63.87,64.51],[64.51,65.23]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Cotton grown by enslaved people made up more than half of U.S. exports.","start":66.2,"end":70.86,"words":[[66.26,66.6],[66.6,66.94],[66.94,67.14],[67.14,67.68],[67.68,68.02],[68.02,68.3],[68.3,68.43],[68.43,68.57],[68.57,68.77],[68.77,68.98],[68.98,69.11],[69.11,69.45],[69.79,70.34]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Northern banks took enslaved people as loan collateral, insurers wrote policies on their lives, and universities profited: in 1838 the Jesuits who ran Georgetown sold 272 people to keep the college afloat.","start":71.21,"end":85.16,"words":[[71.28,71.63],[71.63,71.97],[71.97,72.18],[72.18,72.73],[72.73,73.07],[73.07,73.21],[73.21,73.48],[73.48,74.17],[74.41,74.96],[74.96,75.23],[75.23,75.71],[75.71,75.85],[75.85,76.06],[76.06,76.47],[76.71,76.92],[76.92,77.67],[77.67,78.29],[78.53,78.67],[78.67,79.56],[79.56,79.7],[79.7,80.31],[80.31,80.45],[80.45,80.66],[80.66,81.28],[81.28,81.62],[81.62,82.99],[82.99,83.34],[83.34,83.47],[83.47,83.68],[83.68,83.82],[83.82,84.23],[84.23,84.71]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Written into the law","start":86.06,"end":87.39,"words":[[86.11,86.53],[86.53,86.87],[86.87,87.04],[87.04,87.22]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Constitution counted enslaved people as three-fifths of a person for representation, protected the slave trade until 1808, and required escaped people to be returned.","start":88.09,"end":99.6,"words":[[88.14,88.29],[88.29,89.18],[89.18,89.7],[89.7,90.3],[90.3,90.67],[90.67,90.82],[90.82,91.41],[91.41,91.56],[91.56,91.71],[91.71,92.08],[92.08,92.23],[92.23,93.35],[93.61,94.28],[94.28,94.42],[94.42,94.8],[94.8,95.17],[95.17,95.54],[95.54,96.36],[96.62,96.84],[96.84,97.44],[97.44,97.96],[97.96,98.33],[98.33,98.48],[98.48,98.63],[98.63,99.15]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 forced free states to help capture them.","start":99.95,"end":105.25,"words":[[100.04,100.19],[100.19,100.88],[100.88,101.26],[101.26,101.49],[101.49,101.64],[101.64,102.41],[102.41,102.71],[102.71,102.94],[102.94,103.4],[103.4,103.55],[103.55,103.86],[103.86,104.32],[104.32,104.62]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In Dred Scott v. Sandford (1857) Chief Justice Taney wrote that Black people had “no rights which the white man was bound to respect.”","start":105.6,"end":115.09,"words":[[105.65,105.81],[105.81,106.11],[106.11,106.42],[106.42,106.65],[107.03,107.57],[107.57,108.71],[108.98,109.29],[109.29,109.82],[109.82,110.2],[110.2,110.51],[110.51,110.74],[110.74,111.05],[111.05,111.43],[111.43,111.66],[111.66,111.89],[111.89,112.27],[112.27,112.58],[112.58,112.73],[112.73,113.03],[113.03,113.26],[113.26,113.49],[113.49,113.88],[113.88,114.03],[114.03,114.64]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Did violence drive cotton's productivity?","start":115.99,"end":119.18,"words":[[116.05,116.27],[116.27,116.86],[116.86,117.23],[117.23,117.67],[117.67,118.62]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Historian Edward Baptist (The Half Has Never Been Told, 2014) argued that rising cotton-picking rates came largely from systematic torture and quotas.","start":119.88,"end":131.24,"words":[[119.93,120.68],[120.68,121.1],[121.1,121.68],[121.68,121.85],[121.85,122.1],[122.1,122.35],[122.35,122.68],[122.68,122.93],[122.93,123.43],[123.72,124.64],[124.93,125.34],[125.34,125.59],[125.59,126.09],[126.09,126.92],[126.92,127.34],[127.34,127.67],[127.67,128.17],[128.17,128.51],[128.51,129.34],[129.34,129.76],[129.76,130.0],[130.0,130.67]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Economists Alan Olmstead and Paul Rhode answered that better cotton varieties explain most of the gain.","start":131.59,"end":138.51,"words":[[131.61,132.41],[132.41,132.73],[132.73,133.3],[133.3,133.54],[133.54,133.78],[133.78,134.1],[134.1,134.51],[134.51,134.75],[134.75,135.07],[135.07,135.47],[135.47,136.2],[136.2,136.84],[136.84,137.24],[137.24,137.41],[137.41,137.57],[137.57,137.97]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Both sides agree enslaved people were whipped, sold and separated from their families; the debate is over how much violence drove productivity.","start":138.86,"end":149.08,"words":[[138.91,139.23],[139.23,139.63],[139.63,139.95],[139.95,140.59],[140.59,140.99],[140.99,141.15],[141.15,141.55],[141.83,142.23],[142.23,142.47],[142.47,143.27],[143.27,143.59],[143.59,143.83],[143.83,144.48],[144.76,144.92],[144.92,145.4],[145.4,145.56],[145.56,145.88],[145.88,146.12],[146.12,146.44],[146.44,147.08],[147.08,147.48],[147.48,148.52]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Resistance and freedom","start":149.98,"end":151.61,"words":[[150.03,150.76],[150.76,151.01],[151.01,151.49]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Enslaved people resisted every day, by slowing work, escaping and rebelling: Stono (1739), the German Coast uprising (1811), Nat Turner (1831).","start":152.31,"end":164.43,"words":[[152.36,152.95],[152.95,153.32],[153.32,153.92],[153.92,154.21],[154.21,154.51],[154.77,154.99],[154.99,155.44],[155.44,155.73],[155.99,156.59],[156.59,156.81],[156.81,157.4],[157.66,158.18],[158.18,159.44],[159.7,159.85],[159.85,160.3],[160.3,160.67],[160.67,161.26],[161.26,162.15],[162.41,162.63],[162.63,162.93],[162.93,163.89]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Harriet Tubman made about 13 trips back into Maryland and led about 70 people to freedom.","start":164.78,"end":170.76,"words":[[164.82,165.23],[165.23,165.64],[165.64,165.91],[165.91,166.26],[166.26,166.6],[166.6,166.94],[166.94,167.14],[167.14,167.42],[167.42,168.03],[168.03,168.24],[168.24,168.44],[168.44,168.78],[168.78,169.26],[169.26,169.6],[169.6,169.74],[169.74,170.22]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Maroon communities lived in places like the Great Dismal Swamp.","start":171.11,"end":175.17,"words":[[171.15,171.48],[171.48,172.14],[172.14,172.41],[172.41,172.54],[172.54,173.0],[173.0,173.27],[173.27,173.4],[173.4,173.73],[173.73,174.12],[174.12,174.52]]},{"el":"s4","text":"About 179,000 Black men served in the Union Army.","start":175.52,"end":179.8,"words":[[175.56,175.88],[175.88,177.76],[177.76,178.01],[178.01,178.19],[178.19,178.44],[178.44,178.57],[178.57,178.69],[178.69,179.07],[179.07,179.32]]},{"el":"s4","text":"On June 19, 1865, Union troops announced emancipation in Galveston, Texas; Juneteenth became a federal holiday in 2021.","start":180.15,"end":189.57,"words":[[180.19,180.31],[180.31,180.56],[180.56,181.06],[181.28,182.28],[182.5,182.87],[182.87,183.18],[183.18,183.62],[183.62,184.37],[184.37,184.49],[184.49,185.12],[185.34,185.77],[185.99,186.56],[186.56,186.93],[186.93,187.05],[187.05,187.49],[187.49,187.93],[187.93,188.05],[188.05,189.05]]}]}